    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so im looking at this home contract for a house in seattle and i keep seeing the word 'escrow' everywhere. what actually is an escrow agent and why do they need to hold onto my money for a few weeks? it feels weird just letting it sit there.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Escrow, in a real estate transaction, is a legal arrangement where a neutral third party holds funds and documents until all the conditions of the sale are met. The escrow agent acts as that trusted middleman, ensuring that the buyer and seller both fulfill their obligations without either party getting their money or property before the deal is finalized. They hold your money for a few weeks because that time is necessary to complete all the due diligence, like inspections, appraisals, and title searches, before the final exchange happens. It's a safety net, designed to prevent one party from backing out after the other has already committed resources.",
      "source": "target",
